1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,762
Sweetheart, every year,

2
00:00:01,762 --> 00:00:04,981
people spend eighty dollars on a beautiful

3
00:00:04,981 --> 00:00:06,514
real Christmas tree,

4
00:00:06,514 --> 00:00:09,043
and by the fifteenth of December,

5
00:00:09,043 --> 00:00:12,261
it's dropping needles all over the carpet,

6
00:00:12,261 --> 00:00:14,254
the branches are drooping,

7
00:00:14,254 --> 00:00:16,323
and it smells like nothing.

8
00:00:16,503 --> 00:00:17,949
By Christmas morning,

9
00:00:17,949 --> 00:00:20,222
it looks like it's been through a

10
00:00:20,222 --> 00:00:20,497
war.

11
00:00:20,677 --> 00:00:22,918
And it isn't the tree's fault.

12
00:00:23,098 --> 00:00:25,708
It's five little mistakes people make in

13
00:00:25,708 --> 00:00:27,926
the first hour after they bring it

14
00:00:27,926 --> 00:00:28,253
home.

15
00:00:28,602 --> 00:00:32,026
So tonight I'm giving you seven old-fashioned

16
00:00:32,026 --> 00:00:34,916
tricks that keep a real Christmas tree

17
00:00:34,916 --> 00:00:37,731
fresh, green and fragrant all the way

18
00:00:37,731 --> 00:00:38,796
to New Year's.

19
00:00:38,976 --> 00:00:40,142
And stay with me,

20
00:00:40,142 --> 00:00:42,611
because the last one is something my

21
00:00:42,611 --> 00:00:44,601
father did every single year,

22
00:00:44,601 --> 00:00:47,070
the minute the tree came through the

23
00:00:47,070 --> 00:00:49,402
door, and our trees still had soft

24
00:00:49,402 --> 00:00:51,666
needles in the middle of January.

25
00:00:52,016 --> 00:00:53,373
My father, Walter,

26
00:00:53,373 --> 00:00:56,541
cut our Christmas tree himself every year,

27
00:00:56,541 --> 00:00:59,106
from a farm out on Route Thirteen,

28
00:00:59,106 --> 00:01:01,595
where the farmer let him take one

29
00:01:01,595 --> 00:01:03,858
for a dollar if he helped with

30
00:01:03,858 --> 00:01:05,065
the fence posts.

31
00:01:05,245 --> 00:01:07,686
He'd drag it home on Danny's sled,

32
00:01:07,686 --> 00:01:08,906
through the snow,

33
00:01:08,906 --> 00:01:11,418
and the whole street could smell it

34
00:01:11,418 --> 00:01:11,921
coming.

35
00:01:12,101 --> 00:01:14,626
And that tree would stand in our

36
00:01:14,626 --> 00:01:17,229
front window until Twelfth Night,

37
00:01:17,229 --> 00:01:18,886
the sixth of January,

38
00:01:18,886 --> 00:01:19,833
still green.

39
00:01:20,183 --> 00:01:22,492
A cut tree is still alive for

40
00:01:22,492 --> 00:01:23,527
weeks, honey.

41
00:01:23,707 --> 00:01:26,249
It drinks water through its trunk,

42
00:01:26,249 --> 00:01:27,968
like flowers in a vase.

43
00:01:28,148 --> 00:01:30,621
A good-sized tree can drink a gallon

44
00:01:30,621 --> 00:01:32,270
a day in the first week.

45
00:01:32,450 --> 00:01:34,360
When it stops drinking,

46
00:01:34,360 --> 00:01:35,689
it starts dying.

47
00:01:35,869 --> 00:01:38,241
Every trick I'm going to tell you

48
00:01:38,241 --> 00:01:39,607
is about one thing.

49
00:01:39,787 --> 00:01:41,889
Keeping that tree drinking.

50
00:01:42,069 --> 00:01:43,967
In a minute I'll tell you the

51
00:01:43,967 --> 00:01:46,389
one mistake that stops it drinking in

52
00:01:46,389 --> 00:01:47,502
just a few hours.

53
00:01:47,851 --> 00:01:48,920
Number one.

54
00:01:49,100 --> 00:01:50,412
Choose a fresh one.

55
00:01:50,592 --> 00:01:52,896
Grab a branch and pull your hand

56
00:01:52,896 --> 00:01:54,841
gently along it toward you.

57
00:01:55,021 --> 00:01:57,193
If more than a few needles come

58
00:01:57,193 --> 00:01:58,384
off in your hand,

59
00:01:58,384 --> 00:01:59,084
walk away.

60
00:01:59,264 --> 00:02:01,006
Bend a needle in half.

61
00:02:01,186 --> 00:02:02,717
On a fresh fir,

62
00:02:02,717 --> 00:02:03,635
it bends.

63
00:02:03,815 --> 00:02:05,242
On a dry one,

64
00:02:05,242 --> 00:02:06,230
it snaps.

65
00:02:06,410 --> 00:02:08,462
And lift the tree a few inches

66
00:02:08,462 --> 00:02:10,172
and drop it on its trunk.

67
00:02:10,352 --> 00:02:12,688
If green needles rain down,

68
00:02:12,688 --> 00:02:14,764
it was cut too long ago.

69
00:02:15,113 --> 00:02:17,048
And pick the right kind,

70
00:02:17,048 --> 00:02:17,935
sweetheart.

71
00:02:18,115 --> 00:02:20,553
Fraser fir and noble fir hold their

72
00:02:20,553 --> 00:02:21,946
needles the longest,

73
00:02:21,946 --> 00:02:24,663
and they have strong branches for heavy

74
00:02:24,663 --> 00:02:25,359
ornaments.

75
00:02:25,539 --> 00:02:27,586
Balsam fir smells the best,

76
00:02:27,586 --> 00:02:29,255
like a Christmas card.

77
00:02:29,435 --> 00:02:32,211
Scotch pine is cheapest and holds on

78
00:02:32,211 --> 00:02:33,986
well, but it's prickly.

79
00:02:34,166 --> 00:02:37,685
Spruce smells wonderful but drops its needles

80
00:02:37,685 --> 00:02:38,624
the fastest.

81
00:02:38,804 --> 00:02:41,395
My father always bought balsam,

82
00:02:41,395 --> 00:02:42,565
for the smell.

83
00:02:42,915 --> 00:02:44,854
And here's a little secret,

84
00:02:44,854 --> 00:02:45,644
sweetheart.

85
00:02:45,824 --> 00:02:47,945
The trees at the big box stores

86
00:02:47,945 --> 00:02:50,544
were usually cut in early November and

87
00:02:50,544 --> 00:02:52,391
trucked across the country,

88
00:02:52,391 --> 00:02:54,102
sitting in a hot trailer.

89
00:02:54,282 --> 00:02:56,161
A tree from a local farm,

90
00:02:56,161 --> 00:02:58,642
where you cut it yourself or they

91
00:02:58,642 --> 00:02:59,920
cut it that week,

92
00:02:59,920 --> 00:03:01,724
will last twice as long.

93
00:03:01,904 --> 00:03:03,912
It costs about the same.

94
00:03:04,092 --> 00:03:06,086
And you get a hayride and a

95
00:03:06,086 --> 00:03:07,784
cup of cocoa thrown in.

96
00:03:07,964 --> 00:03:10,875
My grandchildren wouldn't have it any other

97
00:03:10,875 --> 00:03:11,145
way.

98
00:03:11,495 --> 00:03:14,775
Number two, the most important mistake of

99
00:03:14,775 --> 00:03:15,095
all.

100
00:03:15,275 --> 00:03:16,795
Not cutting the trunk.

101
00:03:16,975 --> 00:03:18,522
When a tree is cut,

102
00:03:18,522 --> 00:03:21,372
the sap seals over the stump within

103
00:03:21,372 --> 00:03:22,349
a few hours,

104
00:03:22,349 --> 00:03:23,326
like a scab.

105
00:03:23,506 --> 00:03:25,521
Then it can't drink a drop,

106
00:03:25,521 --> 00:03:27,984
no matter how much water you give

107
00:03:27,984 --> 00:03:28,208
it.

108
00:03:28,388 --> 00:03:30,665
So right before you put it in

109
00:03:30,665 --> 00:03:33,177
the stand, cut a fresh slice off

110
00:03:33,177 --> 00:03:35,689
the bottom, about an inch thick,

111
00:03:35,689 --> 00:03:36,945
straight across.

112
00:03:37,125 --> 00:03:39,519
Most tree lots will do it for

113
00:03:39,519 --> 00:03:40,840
free if you ask.

114
00:03:41,189 --> 00:03:43,423
And get it into water within a

115
00:03:43,423 --> 00:03:45,062
few hours of that cut,

116
00:03:45,062 --> 00:03:45,508
honey.

117
00:03:45,688 --> 00:03:47,721
If you can't put it up right

118
00:03:47,721 --> 00:03:49,826
away, stand it in a bucket of

119
00:03:49,826 --> 00:03:51,786
water in the garage or on a

120
00:03:51,786 --> 00:03:52,585
cold porch.

121
00:03:52,765 --> 00:03:54,485
And cut straight across,

122
00:03:54,485 --> 00:03:56,348
not at an angle and not in

123
00:03:56,348 --> 00:03:56,921
a point.

124
00:03:57,101 --> 00:03:59,386
A flat cut sits in the water

125
00:03:59,386 --> 00:03:59,957
evenly.

126
00:04:00,137 --> 00:04:02,429
And don't drill holes in the trunk

127
00:04:02,429 --> 00:04:02,900
either.

128
00:04:03,080 --> 00:04:05,904
The old wives' tales are wrong about

129
00:04:05,904 --> 00:04:06,296
that.

130
00:04:06,646 --> 00:04:07,982
Number three.

131
00:04:08,162 --> 00:04:10,298
A stand that's too small.

132
00:04:10,478 --> 00:04:12,501
If the stand only holds a cup

133
00:04:12,501 --> 00:04:14,803
of water, your tree will drink it

134
00:04:14,803 --> 00:04:16,128
dry in a few hours,

135
00:04:16,128 --> 00:04:17,732
the sap will seal over,

136
00:04:17,732 --> 00:04:18,988
and it's finished.

137
00:04:19,168 --> 00:04:21,399
Get a stand that holds at least

138
00:04:21,399 --> 00:04:22,047
a gallon.

139
00:04:22,227 --> 00:04:23,934
The rule is a quart of water

140
00:04:23,934 --> 00:04:25,884
for every inch across the trunk.

141
00:04:26,064 --> 00:04:28,514
A four-inch trunk needs a gallon.

142
00:04:28,694 --> 00:04:31,847
My father used an old galvanized wash

143
00:04:31,847 --> 00:04:33,721
tub filled with rocks.

144
00:04:34,071 --> 00:04:35,862
If this is helping you,

145
00:04:35,862 --> 00:04:37,964
tap the like button for me,

146
00:04:37,964 --> 00:04:38,820
sweetheart.

147
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:41,635
It helps Grandma more than you know.

148
00:04:41,815 --> 00:04:44,509
And check the water every single day,

149
00:04:44,509 --> 00:04:46,401
especially the first week.

150
00:04:46,581 --> 00:04:48,149
Stick your finger in.

151
00:04:48,329 --> 00:04:50,928
If the water drops below the bottom

152
00:04:50,928 --> 00:04:52,636
of the trunk even once,

153
00:04:52,636 --> 00:04:54,418
the stump can seal over,

154
00:04:54,418 --> 00:04:56,943
and you'll have to start all over.

155
00:04:57,123 --> 00:04:59,647
I keep a watering can right behind

156
00:04:59,647 --> 00:05:01,280
the tree all December.

157
00:05:01,629 --> 00:05:04,032
Here's a trick for checking the water

158
00:05:04,032 --> 00:05:06,563
without crawling under the tree on your

159
00:05:06,563 --> 00:05:07,602
hands and knees,

160
00:05:07,602 --> 00:05:07,992
honey.

161
00:05:08,172 --> 00:05:10,398
Put a long funnel with a piece

162
00:05:10,398 --> 00:05:13,440
of plastic tubing right behind the trunk,

163
00:05:13,440 --> 00:05:15,147
tucked in the branches,

164
00:05:15,147 --> 00:05:17,077
going down into the stand.

165
00:05:17,257 --> 00:05:19,812
You can pour water down it standing

166
00:05:19,812 --> 00:05:20,031
up.

167
00:05:20,211 --> 00:05:22,914
My son-in-law rigged one up for me

168
00:05:22,914 --> 00:05:24,662
when my knees got bad,

169
00:05:24,662 --> 00:05:27,444
and I've used it for fifteen years.

170
00:05:27,794 --> 00:05:28,979
Number four.

171
00:05:29,159 --> 00:05:31,237
Putting things in the water.

172
00:05:31,417 --> 00:05:33,672
People add aspirin,

173
00:05:33,672 --> 00:05:35,571
sugar, soda pop,

174
00:05:35,571 --> 00:05:37,826
bleach, even vodka.

175
00:05:38,006 --> 00:05:39,608
I've heard them all.

176
00:05:39,788 --> 00:05:43,106
The Christmas tree growers and the agricultural

177
00:05:43,106 --> 00:05:45,860
colleges have tested every one of them.

178
00:05:46,040 --> 00:05:48,499
Plain tap water works as well as

179
00:05:48,499 --> 00:05:50,881
anything, and better than most.

180
00:05:51,061 --> 00:05:52,884
Sugar just grows mold.

181
00:05:53,064 --> 00:05:55,680
Save the aspirin for your headache,

182
00:05:55,680 --> 00:05:56,129
honey.

183
00:05:56,309 --> 00:05:58,492
Plain, cool water.

184
00:05:58,672 --> 00:05:59,844
That's all.

185
00:06:00,194 --> 00:06:02,313
So far, a fresh tree,

186
00:06:02,313 --> 00:06:03,523
a fresh cut,

187
00:06:03,523 --> 00:06:04,734
a big stand,

188
00:06:04,734 --> 00:06:06,348
and plain water.

189
00:06:06,528 --> 00:06:07,875
Number five.

190
00:06:08,055 --> 00:06:10,086
Putting the tree next to the heat.

191
00:06:10,266 --> 00:06:12,032
Not by the fireplace,

192
00:06:12,032 --> 00:06:14,303
not next to a heating vent,

193
00:06:14,303 --> 00:06:16,153
not by a sunny window.

194
00:06:16,333 --> 00:06:18,900
Heat dries a tree out twice as

195
00:06:18,900 --> 00:06:19,328
fast.

196
00:06:19,508 --> 00:06:21,541
Put it in the coolest corner of

197
00:06:21,541 --> 00:06:22,656
the room you can,

198
00:06:22,656 --> 00:06:24,623
and if you have a vent nearby,

199
00:06:24,623 --> 00:06:26,591
close it or put a deflector on

200
00:06:26,591 --> 00:06:26,787
it.

201
00:06:26,967 --> 00:06:28,728
Turn the heat down a little at

202
00:06:28,728 --> 00:06:29,080
night.

203
00:06:29,430 --> 00:06:31,601
And this is a safety rule,

204
00:06:31,601 --> 00:06:32,937
too, sweetheart.

205
00:06:33,117 --> 00:06:36,128
A dry Christmas tree catches fire in

206
00:06:36,128 --> 00:06:36,797
seconds.

207
00:06:36,977 --> 00:06:38,916
It goes up like a torch.

208
00:06:39,096 --> 00:06:40,290
Use LED lights,

209
00:06:40,290 --> 00:06:41,563
which stay cool,

210
00:06:41,563 --> 00:06:44,189
and turn the tree lights off when

211
00:06:44,189 --> 00:06:46,258
you go to bed or leave the

212
00:06:46,258 --> 00:06:46,735
house.

213
00:06:46,915 --> 00:06:49,616
Never put candles on or near the

214
00:06:49,616 --> 00:06:50,038
tree.

215
00:06:50,218 --> 00:06:52,791
My mother's friend lost her house in

216
00:06:52,791 --> 00:06:55,579
nineteen sixty-four to an old string of

217
00:06:55,579 --> 00:06:57,080
lights on a dry tree.

218
00:06:57,429 --> 00:06:59,821
And put a waterproof tray or a

219
00:06:59,821 --> 00:07:02,851
plastic boot mat under your tree stand

220
00:07:02,851 --> 00:07:05,243
before the tree skirt goes on.

221
00:07:05,423 --> 00:07:07,689
A stand always leaks a little,

222
00:07:07,689 --> 00:07:10,181
and a full gallon spilled under a

223
00:07:10,181 --> 00:07:12,371
tree can ruin a wood floor or

224
00:07:12,371 --> 00:07:13,051
a carpet.

225
00:07:13,231 --> 00:07:16,105
One year, Harold knocked the stand while

226
00:07:16,105 --> 00:07:17,614
adjusting the lights,

227
00:07:17,614 --> 00:07:19,697
and the water ran all the way

228
00:07:19,697 --> 00:07:20,847
under the couch.

229
00:07:21,027 --> 00:07:23,388
We found the puddle on New Year's

230
00:07:23,388 --> 00:07:23,674
Day.

231
00:07:24,023 --> 00:07:25,359
Number six.

232
00:07:25,539 --> 00:07:27,814
A little drink for the branches.

233
00:07:27,994 --> 00:07:30,514
Fill a spray bottle with water,

234
00:07:30,514 --> 00:07:32,545
and once or twice a week,

235
00:07:32,545 --> 00:07:34,008
mist the branches,

236
00:07:34,008 --> 00:07:36,284
the outside ones especially,

237
00:07:36,284 --> 00:07:38,722
before you turn the lights on.

238
00:07:38,902 --> 00:07:41,402
Never spray it while the lights are

239
00:07:41,402 --> 00:07:42,617
on or plugged in.

240
00:07:42,797 --> 00:07:45,536
It keeps the needles from drying out,

241
00:07:45,536 --> 00:07:48,126
and it brings the smell right back.

242
00:07:48,306 --> 00:07:50,816
My mother did it every Sunday morning

243
00:07:50,816 --> 00:07:51,766
before church.

244
00:07:52,116 --> 00:07:53,359
And number seven,

245
00:07:53,359 --> 00:07:55,041
the one I promised you.

246
00:07:55,221 --> 00:07:57,311
My father's first hour.

247
00:07:57,491 --> 00:07:59,376
When he brought the tree home,

248
00:07:59,376 --> 00:08:01,700
before it ever came inside the house,

249
00:08:01,700 --> 00:08:03,396
he cut a fresh inch off the

250
00:08:03,396 --> 00:08:05,280
trunk and stood it in a bucket

251
00:08:05,280 --> 00:08:07,416
of lukewarm water in the cold shed

252
00:08:07,416 --> 00:08:08,044
overnight.

253
00:08:08,224 --> 00:08:11,217
He swore lukewarm water got it drinking

254
00:08:11,217 --> 00:08:11,754
faster.

255
00:08:11,934 --> 00:08:14,717
The tree experts say any water works,

256
00:08:14,717 --> 00:08:16,823
honey, but I still do it his

257
00:08:16,823 --> 00:08:17,124
way.

258
00:08:17,304 --> 00:08:19,250
And a night in the cold lets

259
00:08:19,250 --> 00:08:21,543
the tree rest before it meets the

260
00:08:21,543 --> 00:08:22,377
heat inside.

261
00:08:22,728 --> 00:08:24,302
Then the next morning,

262
00:08:24,302 --> 00:08:26,664
he cut another thin slice off the

263
00:08:26,664 --> 00:08:29,455
bottom, put it straight into the stand,

264
00:08:29,455 --> 00:08:31,888
and filled it with lukewarm water.

265
00:08:32,068 --> 00:08:34,820
That tree would drink almost two gallons

266
00:08:34,820 --> 00:08:35,783
the first day.

267
00:08:35,963 --> 00:08:38,392
We had to refill it at breakfast,

268
00:08:38,392 --> 00:08:39,643
lunch and supper.

269
00:08:39,823 --> 00:08:42,517
And it never dropped a needle until

270
00:08:42,517 --> 00:08:43,132
January.

271
00:08:43,312 --> 00:08:45,786
People on Elm Street used to ask

272
00:08:45,786 --> 00:08:46,946
him his secret.

273
00:08:47,126 --> 00:08:48,234
He'd just say,

274
00:08:48,234 --> 00:08:49,500
give it a drink,

275
00:08:49,500 --> 00:08:51,875
same as you would a tired man.

276
00:08:52,225 --> 00:08:54,328
And when the season's over,

277
00:08:54,328 --> 00:08:56,743
don't just drag it to the curb,

278
00:08:56,743 --> 00:08:57,600
sweetheart.

279
00:08:57,780 --> 00:09:00,582
Many towns collect trees and grind them

280
00:09:00,582 --> 00:09:02,378
into mulch for the parks.

281
00:09:02,558 --> 00:09:04,626
Or put it in your backyard,

282
00:09:04,626 --> 00:09:06,849
and the birds will use it for

283
00:09:06,849 --> 00:09:08,304
shelter all winter.

284
00:09:08,484 --> 00:09:10,627
My father stood ours up in the

285
00:09:10,627 --> 00:09:13,340
snow by the bird feeder every January,

286
00:09:13,340 --> 00:09:16,340
and hung pinecones dipped in peanut butter

287
00:09:16,340 --> 00:09:17,482
on its branches.

288
00:09:17,662 --> 00:09:19,404
The cardinals loved it.

289
00:09:19,754 --> 00:09:21,263
So one more time.

290
00:09:21,443 --> 00:09:23,901
Choose a fresh tree with the needle

291
00:09:23,901 --> 00:09:24,252
test.

292
00:09:24,432 --> 00:09:26,280
Cut an inch off the trunk,

293
00:09:26,280 --> 00:09:27,416
straight across.

294
00:09:27,596 --> 00:09:28,979
Use a big stand,

295
00:09:28,979 --> 00:09:30,449
a gallon or more,

296
00:09:30,449 --> 00:09:32,438
and check it every day.

297
00:09:32,618 --> 00:09:33,953
Plain water.

298
00:09:34,133 --> 00:09:36,102
Keep it away from the heat,

299
00:09:36,102 --> 00:09:38,510
and turn the lights off at night.

300
00:09:38,690 --> 00:09:40,106
Mist the branches.

301
00:09:40,286 --> 00:09:41,872
And give it a night in a

302
00:09:41,872 --> 00:09:44,514
bucket of lukewarm water before it comes

303
00:09:44,514 --> 00:09:44,977
inside.

304
00:09:45,157 --> 00:09:47,183
Green until New Year's,

305
00:09:47,183 --> 00:09:48,152
sweetheart.

306
00:09:48,332 --> 00:09:49,504
I promise.

307
00:09:49,855 --> 00:09:51,796
Tell me in the comments,

308
00:09:51,796 --> 00:09:52,281
honey.

309
00:09:52,461 --> 00:09:55,294
Real tree or artificial in your house?

310
00:09:55,474 --> 00:09:57,773
And did your father have a secret

311
00:09:57,773 --> 00:09:58,051
too?

312
00:09:58,231 --> 00:10:01,567
The whole playlist of Grandma's Christmas tips

313
00:10:01,567 --> 00:10:03,742
is right there on your screen,

314
00:10:03,742 --> 00:10:05,772
and I'll see you in the next

315
00:10:05,772 --> 00:10:06,207
video.

316
00:10:06,387 --> 00:10:08,315
Good night, sweetheart.
